== Cluster audit ==

Wave 14 does not use the prior “miscellaneous durable seeds” slot as a dumping ground. The promoted concepts form one connected public cluster around:

<pre>
research communication
→ evaluation / citation / publishing
→ access / openness
→ data stewardship / reuse
</pre>

The cluster connects directly to existing epistemology, provenance, metadata, scientific-method, reproducibility, and computational-research foundations.

== Publication / evaluation audit ==

The corpus preserves:

<pre>
scientific literature ≠ all scientific knowledge
peer review ≠ proof of correctness
peer review ≠ replication
citation ≠ endorsement
citation count ≠ quality
academic publishing ≠ peer review
preprint ≠ peer-reviewed article
publication ≠ correctness
</pre>

Publication state and review state are therefore provenance, not truth labels.

== Open-research audit ==

[[Open access]] remains a publication-access/reuse concept.

[[Open science]] is broader and includes data, software, methods, infrastructure, participation, and research-process openness where appropriate.

The corpus explicitly preserves:

<pre>
open access ≠ open science
free-to-read ≠ unrestricted reuse necessarily
open science ≠ compulsory disclosure of all research
openness ≠ reproducibility automatically
</pre>

Ethical, legal, privacy, security, cultural, and technical restrictions remain legitimate constraints on openness.

== Data / repository audit ==

[[Dataset]] remains distinct from [[Database]].

[[Research data management]] covers the lifecycle and is broader than data sharing.

[[Data repository]] is a stewardship/deposit/preservation/discovery service rather than a synonym for database storage.

The corpus preserves:

<pre>
dataset ≠ database
dataset ≠ raw data necessarily
research data management ≠ sharing only
data repository ≠ database
repository deposit ≠ preservation guarantee
</pre>

== FAIR audit ==

[[FAIR data]] follows Wilkinson et al. (2016): Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, Reusable.

Required non-equivalences are explicit:

<pre>
FAIR ≠ open
FAIR ≠ data quality
FAIR ≠ one implementation standard
</pre>

Controlled-access data can remain FAIR when identifiers, metadata, access procedures, and reuse conditions are machine/actionably described.

== Current-source audit ==

* UNESCO Recommendation on Open Science — adopted 23 November 2021.
* NIH Data Management and Sharing Policy — effective 25 January 2023 and still the underlying policy.
* NIH 2026 DMS change — simplified required plan format; not a replacement policy.
* NIH/NLM preprint definition — preprints remain public scientific drafts not yet certified by peer review.
* Wilkinson et al. FAIR Principles — 2016 foundational publication; GO FAIR clarification used for FAIR ≠ open.
* Crossref — current reference/citation metadata practice.

== Cross-wave audit ==

Wave 14 reuses [[Evidence]], [[Provenance]], [[Verification]], [[Validation]], [[Reproducibility]], [[Replication]], [[Metadata]], [[Database]], [[Knowledge graph]], [[Scientific method]], [[Version control]], [[Interoperability]], and [[Governance]] without duplicating their identities.

[[Reproducible computational research]] remains a concrete computational practice; [[Open science]] and [[Research data management]] are broader institutional/research-lifecycle concepts.

== Domain audit ==

No new controlled Domain is required. Wave 14 uses Science, Computer science, Technology, and Systems science.

